# Statistical Analysis
In this study, we are investigating the difference in broadband usage rates between Los Angeles and New York City. We will construct a two-sided hypothesis 

## Parameters of Interest

The parameters of interest are the true mean broadband usage rates:

- $\mu_{LA}$: The true mean broadband usage rate for ZIP codes in Los Angeles
- $\mu_{NYC}$: The true mean broadband usage rate for ZIP codes in New York City

## Type of Inference
We used a two-sample $t$-test for independent samples with unequal variances (Welch’s $t$-test) to test for a significant difference between the two population means.

## Assumptions
$$X = \text{Broadband Usage for Los Angeles ZIP codes} \sim D_X(\mu_{LA}, \sigma_{LA})$$
$$Y = \text{Broadband Usage for New York City ZIP codes} \sim D_Y(\mu_{NYC}, \sigma_{NYC})$$
Independence: It is assumed that the two groups (Los Angeles and New York City) are independent of each other. This assumption is satisfied as the data were collected from geographically distinct metropolitan areas.Normality: Given our large sample sizes ($n_{LA} = 281, n_{NYC} = 181$), the $t$-test is robust to deviations from normality via the Central Limit Theorem.

Normality: 

## Hypothesis

$$H_0: \mu_{LA} - \mu_{NYC} = 0$$
$$H_{a}: \mu_{LA} - \mu_{NYC} \neq 0$$

## Test Statistic and Null Distribution

The test statistic is calculated as:$$t = \frac{\bar{x} - \bar{y}}{\sqrt{\frac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \frac{s_y^2}{n_y}}}$$

```{r calculations, echo = FALSE}

results <- t.test(usage ~ Location, data = cleaned_data, alternative = "two.sided")

t_stat <- round(results$statistic, 5)
df_w <- round(results$parameter, 5)
p_val <- format(results$p.value, scientific = TRUE)


```

## Results

```{r, echo = FALSE}
t_stat
df_w
p_val
```




## Interpretation: Comparing p-value with alpha = 0.05

**Reject the null hypothesis**: We have enough evidence that the average broadband usage in Los Angeles is significantly different from the average broadband usage in New York City. (p-value = $6.95 \times 10^{-6}$, two-sided test)


## Confidence Interval

```{r, echo = FALSE}
cat("95% Confidence Interval:", results$conf.int[1], "to", results$conf.int[2])
t.test(usage ~ Location, data = cleaned_data, alternative = "two.sided")
```























